quared bishop to its most active square. This move:\n- Develops a piece toward the center\n- Attacks the vulnerable f7 square (only defended by Black\'s king)\n- Prepares for potential kingside castling\n- Is a fundamental move in the Italian Game, one of the oldest and most reliable openings\n- Maintains flexibility for future plans while maintaining central pressure\n\nThis is superior to N: f3-g5 which is premature without more development, and better than d2-d4 which would require concrete calculation of pawn trades.\n\nAnswer: B: f1-c4'}
