es.\n\n5. **N: g5-h7** - Attacks Black's rook on h8, but this seems less forcing.\n\nLooking more carefully at **N: g5-e6**, this is a discovered attack! The knight moves to e6, forking the Black queen on d8 and king on f8, while my bishop on f7 would be giving check to the king. This is a devastating combination - Black would be in check from the bishop and the queen would be attacked by the knight simultaneously.\n\nReason: N: g5-e6 is a powerful tactical blow. After this move, Black's king on f8 is in check from my bishop on f7, and simultaneously my knight on e6 attacks Black's queen on d8. Black must deal with the check first, and will likely lose the queen or face severe material/positional disadvantage. This is a classic example of a royal fork combined with discovered check.\n\nAnswer: N: g5-e6"}
