\n1 |RWF0|NWF0|BWF0|    |    |RWF1|KWF1|    | 1\n  +----+----+----+----+----+----+----+----+\n     a    b    c    d    e    f    g    h \nYour valid moves are (src-dst-promotion, or src-dst):\n[P: a2-a4, P: a2-a3, N: b1-a3, N: b1-c3, N: b1-d2, P: b2-b4, P: b2-b3, B: c1-d2, B: c1-e3, B: c1-f4, B: c1-g5, B: c1-h6, P: c2-c4, P: c2-c3, P: e4-d5, R: f1-e1, R: f1-d1, P: f2-f4, P: f2-f3, K: g1-h1, P: g2-g4, P: g2-g3, P: h2-h4, P: h2-h3]\nYour answer format should be: Answer: piece-name: move. \nPiece-name and move (src-dst-promotion or src-dst) should be directly copied EXACTLY from valid move list.\nExample of valid output format: N: a1-b3; P: e7-e8-q; Piece B: g5-f4.\nPlease select a move from your valid moves that maximize your probability of winning.\n', 'reasoning': '', 'output': "Situation: I'm playing as White in a complex middlegame position. Black's king has been forced to move (KBF1 on d8), losing castling rights. Black has a somewhat cramped position with pieces on the kingside (bishop on e7, knight on d5, pawns on e6, e5, g7). My king has also moved (KWF1 on g1) and my rook has moved to f1. I have a central pawn on e4 and development possibilities with my knight on b1 and bishop on c1.\n\nEvaluation: \n1. **e4-d5**: Captures Black's centralized knight on d5, removing a strong piece and opening lines. This is tactically forcing and wins material.\n2. **B: c1-g5**: Develops the bishop actively, potentially pinning or pressuring Black's kingside, but doesn't create immediate threats.\n3. **N: b1-c3**: Develops the knight to a central square, supporting e4 and preparing further piece coordination.\n4. **P: f2-f4**: Advances on the kingside, but weakens my king position and seems premature.\n\nThe key tactical opportunity is capturing the knight on d5. Black's knight is centrally placed and actively defending. Removing it with e4xd5 is clearly the strongest move, winning a piece (knight) for a pawn, which is a significant material advantage.\n\nReason: Capturing the